 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Boundary Conditions:** The Y-axis inversion formula assumes a specific coordinate system orientation. Incorrect inversion leads to flipped geometry [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Boundary Conditions:** The Y-axis inversion formula assumes a specific coordinate system orientation [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Precision:** Floating-point arithmetic in the offset calculation may introduce small errors, potentially causing gaps or overlaps between adjacent voxel cubes [G].
 *   **Inactive Voxels:** Primitives corresponding to inactive voxels are discarded, which may result in non-contiguous primitive streams if not handled by the rasterizer or subsequent stages [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of primitives (voxels) in the input stream. Each primitive is processed independently in the geometry shader.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ per primitive, plus the storage for the flag volume texture.
-*   **Performance:** Performance depends on the number of active voxels and the complexity of the geometry shader. The use of a flag volume allows for efficient culling of inactive voxels, reducing the number of cubes generated [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation, efficient culling of empty space, flexible support for solid and wireframe rendering [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Generates a large number of vertices (24 per active voxel), which can be expensive for high-resolution volumes. The geometry shader approach may not be as efficient as other voxel rendering techniques (e.g., ray marching) for very large datasets [G].
-*   **When to Use:** Suitable for interactive applications where the volume resolution is moderate and the number of active voxels is sparse. Also useful for debugging or visualization of voxel boundaries [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Not suitable for high-resolution volumes with dense active voxels due to vertex generation overhead. Not ideal for real-time rendering on hardware with limited geometry shader throughput [G].
